{
  "summary": "Stores multipath descriptor relationship metadata in the wallet for both imported and automatically generated descriptors. Updates `WalletDescriptor` to persist the sibling `ScriptPubKeyMan` IDs associated with each expansion, allowing the wallet to reconstruct the original multipath descriptor using `ReconstructMultipath`. Exposes the reconstructed string via an optional `multipath_descriptor` field in `listdescriptors` and includes it in `exportwatchonly` backups.",
  "problem": "When multipath descriptors are imported into a wallet, they are expanded into separate descriptors and stored independently without recording their relationship. Users coordinating with hardware wallets that require policy registration cannot retrieve the exact registered multipath descriptor from the wallet via RPC or backups.",
  "evidence": [
    "PR description: 'This is useful for working with hardware wallets that require policy registration, particularly those that require resending the descriptor that was registered.'"
  ],
  "dependencies": {
    "depends_on": [
      "#36442",
      "#36443"
    ],
    "enables": []
  },
  "scope_notes": "Stacked PR; the branch includes commits from prerequisite PRs #36442 and #36443 modifying BIP32 key paths and descriptor parsing. The wallet-specific changes begin with commit b97c3f4daf.",
  "changed_since_previous": "Unchanged in approach; rebased with minor test updates.",
  "card": "This pull request stores multipath descriptor relationships in the wallet database and exposes the reconstructed multipath descriptor in listdescriptors and watch-only exports. Currently, Bitcoin Core expands multipath descriptors on import and discards their relationship, preventing users from retrieving the registered multipath descriptor needed for hardware signer policy verification. By saving relative ScriptPubKeyMan IDs in WalletDescriptor, the wallet can reconstruct and report the original multipath strings. It depends on #36442 and #36443.",
